WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.533
Pandas.

2
00:00:01.833 --> 00:00:05.327
Before you evaluate any model, your data lands in a table.

3
00:00:05.327 --> 00:00:07.133
Let's learn to interrogate it.

4
00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:11.267
A DataFrame is a table with labeled columns.

5
00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:18.767
Underneath, column operations are vectorized NumPy, so the same instinct applies: whole columns, never loops.

6
00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:22.100
Four moves cover most of what you'll do.

7
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.567
Filtering rows with a boolean mask.

8
00:00:24.867 --> 00:00:26.800
Group by, to summarize groups.

9
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.200
Merge, for joins between frames.

10
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.100
And handling missing values.

11
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.133
Group by deserves a closer look.

12
00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:38.000
First, split the rows into groups by a key, such as the date.

13
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.233
Then apply an aggregate to each group: a count, a max, a mean.

14
00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:45.600
Finally combine the answers into one small table.

15
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.167
Here they all are in code.

16
00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:51.533
A boolean mask keeps only the large asteroids.

17
00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:56.367
Group by date, aggregate a count and a max, and reset the index to combine.

18
00:00:56.667 --> 00:01:00.900
Then merge in a second table, and fill any missing flare values with quiet.

19
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.933
Here's what one groupby can tell you.

20
00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:08.666
Your eval run lands in a DataFrame, with one row per test question.

21
00:01:08.666 --> 00:01:12.900
Grouping by question type and averaging faithfulness gives this.

22
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:19.759
Numeric lookup questions score zero point six one, while everything else sits above zero point eight five.

23
00:01:19.759 --> 00:01:22.420
That's exactly where the system needs work.

24
00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:25.267
The bars for the other types are illustrative.

25
00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:27.322
Let's recap.

26
00:01:27.322 --> 00:01:29.533
A DataFrame is a labeled table.

27
00:01:29.833 --> 00:01:31.533
Filter with a boolean mask.

28
00:01:31.833 --> 00:01:34.033
Group by is split, apply, combine.

29
00:01:34.333 --> 00:01:37.427
Merge joins frames, and fillna repairs gaps.

30
00:01:37.427 --> 00:01:38.833
Next up: matplotlib.
